# Basis transforms preserving real-rootedness and PF

A sequence $B=(B_n)_{n\ge0}$ of real polynomials defines the linear **basis
transform** $T_B$ on $\mathbb R[x]$ by $T_B(x^n)=B_n$, that is,
$T_B\bigl(\sum_n a_n x^n\bigr)=\sum_n a_n B_n$. We ask which transforms in an
explicit family send real-rooted polynomials to real-rooted polynomials, or send
Pólya frequency (PF) polynomials to PF polynomials. Here a polynomial is PF if
it is zero or has nonnegative coefficients and only real, nonpositive roots, and
"real-rooted" allows the zero polynomial in the conclusion. Each family below
turns the question into a short list of explicit parameter inequalities, and the
equivalences are proved in both directions.

## Newton bases

For parameters $c=(c_j)_{j\ge0}$ let
$$
N_n^{c}(x)=\prod_{j=0}^{n-1}(x+c_j),
$$
and let $T_c$ be the basis transform with $T_c(x^n)=N_n^{c}$. The following are
equivalent:

1. $T_c$ sends every real-rooted polynomial with only nonpositive roots to a
   real-rooted polynomial (or zero);
2. $T_c\bigl((x+r)^n\bigr)$ is real-rooted for all $n\ge0$ and all $r\ge0$
   (the *repeated-root tests*);
3. $c$ is a nondecreasing arithmetic progression: $c_j=c_0+jh$ for some
   $h\ge0$.

So a Newton basis preserves real-rootedness on nonpositive-rooted inputs only if
it is a rising-factorial basis $x^n\mapsto\prod_{j<n}(x+c_0+jh)$; the converse
direction reuses the generalized rising-factorial preserver theorem. The
finite-degree version, for $N\ge2$, restricts to inputs of degree at most $N$,
to the tests with $n\le N$, and to the progression condition for $j<N$.

## Order-two recurrence bases

Let $a\ge0$, $b>0$ and $s\ge0$, and define $B_0=1$, $B_1=s$ and
$$
B_{n+2}=a\,B_{n+1}+(bx+c)\,B_n .
$$
The following are equivalent:

1. $T_B$ sends every PF polynomial to a PF polynomial;
2. every row $B_n$ is a PF polynomial;
3. $B_n(0)\ge0$ for all $n$;
4. $a^2+4c\ge0$ and ($2s\ge a$ or $c\ge s^2-as$).

The constant terms $u_n=B_n(0)$ satisfy the scalar recurrence
$u_{n+2}=a\,u_{n+1}+c\,u_n$, $u_0=1$, $u_1=s$, and condition 4 is the sharp
discriminant and initial-value criterion for this scalar sequence to stay
nonnegative. Restricting the inputs to PF polynomials with roots in $[-R,0]$,
for any $R\ge0$, gives the same criterion, because monomials already witness the
necessity. No finite degree cap suffices: for every $N$ there is $c<-\tfrac14$
(with $a=b=s=1$) such that all PF inputs of degree at most $N$ are mapped to PF
polynomials, while some PF polynomial is not.

## Ray semigroups

For $a,b\in\mathbb R$ and $t\ge0$ let $T_t$ be the basis transform with
$T_t(x^n)=B_n(t)$, where $B_0=1$ and
$$
B_{n+1}=\bigl(x+t(a+bn)\bigr)B_n+bt\,x\,B_n' .
$$
This is the one-parameter family generated by $aD+bxD^2$,
$T_t=\exp\bigl(t(aD+bxD^2)\bigr)$ with $D=d/dx$, and it satisfies the semigroup
law $T_{s+t}=T_sT_t$ and $T_0=\mathrm{id}$. Then
$$
\bigl(T_t\text{ preserves PF polynomials for every }t\ge0\bigr)
\iff a\ge0\text{ and }b\ge0 .
$$
Moreover, the finite logarithm $\ell=\sum_{j=1}^{2}\frac{(-1)^{j+1}}{j}(T_1-1)^j$ of
the time-one map recovers the two generator parameters: $\ell(x)=a$, and
$\ell(x^2)=2(a+b)\,x$, so half the coefficient of $x$ in $\ell(x^2)$, minus
$a$, equals $b$. Hence the same equivalence can be tested by two inequalities
on this degree-two logarithm.

## Interval and ray operators

Fix $L<U$, $\rho,\kappa>0$ and $\alpha,\beta\in\mathbb R$, and let
$$
\mathcal D f=\rho\bigl((\alpha x+\beta)f+\kappa(x-L)(x-U)f'\bigr).
$$
Take a seed $g$ with positive leading coefficient whose roots all lie in
$[L,U]$, and let $T$ be the basis transform with $T(x^n)=\mathcal D^n g$, that
is, $Tp=p(\mathcal D)g$. Assume the top-coefficient condition
$\alpha+\kappa\deg g>0$; the slope $\alpha$ may be negative. Write $m_L$ and
$m_U$ for the multiplicities of $L$ and $U$ as roots of $g$. Then for every set
$S\subseteq\mathbb R$, the map $T$ sends every nonzero real-rooted polynomial with
all roots in $S$ to a nonzero real-rooted polynomial with all roots in $[L,U]$ if
and only if
$$
S\subseteq\Bigl[\rho\bigl(\alpha L+\beta-\kappa(U-L)m_L\bigr),\
\rho\bigl(\alpha U+\beta+\kappa(U-L)m_U\bigr)\Bigr].
$$
The ray analogue uses $\mathcal Df=(a+bx)f+\kappa(x-h)f'$ with $b>0$ and
$\kappa\ge0$ and a seed whose roots lie in $(-\infty,h]$: the exact source ray is
$S\subseteq(-\infty,\,a+bh+\kappa\,m_h]$, where $m_h$ is the multiplicity of $h$
as a root of $g$. Both statements are proved by factoring $p$ into linear factors
and observing that $(\mathcal D-\xi)$ is a first-order differential step that
keeps the roots in the target interval exactly when $\xi$ lies in the window.

## Appell translation rigidity

Let $P_n$ be monic of degree $n$ and suppose $T_P$ sends every real-rooted
polynomial with only nonpositive roots to a real-rooted polynomial (or zero).
Define the *Appell coefficients*
$$
A_k=\frac1{k!}\sum_{j=0}^{k}\binom kj(-x)^{k-j}P_j ,
$$
so that $P_n=\sum_k n(n-1)\cdots(n-k+1)\,x^{n-k}A_k$, equivalently
$T_Pp=\sum_k A_k\,p^{(k)}$. Then $\deg A_k\le\lfloor k/2\rfloor$. Put
$\beta=2\,[x^1]A_2$. The following are equivalent:

1. $\beta=0$;
2. every $A_k$ is constant;
3. $T_P$ commutes with every translation, $T_P(p(x+r))=(T_Pp)(x+r)$;
4. $P_n'=nP_{n-1}$ for all $n$, that is, $(P_n)$ is an Appell sequence.

In this case $T_P$ preserves every real-rooted input, not only those with
nonpositive roots. In general the even top coefficients satisfy the sharp bounds
$$
0\le[x^j]A_{2j}\le\frac{\bigl([x^1]A_2\bigr)^j}{j!},
$$
which are attained by exponential coefficient data $[x^j]A_{2j}=c^j/j!$.

## What is new

The statements on this page were developed in this library; the formalization
cites no source for them, so we attribute none. In particular the order-two criterion is tracked in
Issue 2186 of the repository. The classical background is the study of
real-rootedness preservers and multiplier sequences (see the
[multiplier sequences](/RealRooted/concepts/multiplier-sequences/) page), and the
[Narayana polynomials](/RealRooted/families/narayana/) page uses the basis-transform
API for rising and falling factorial bases.

## References

No published source is cited for these results in the formalization, and we do
not claim here that they are new in the literature. For the definition of PF
polynomials and the background on preservers, see the pages linked above.
